# Target C — bounded multiplicity of deep isolate blockers

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** proof-only inactive-container theorem; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

Canonical Section 87 supplies, for every fixed depth `w>1`, a linear family of
vertex-disjoint contact-isolate pairs

\[
M=\{\{x_i,y_i\}:1\le i\le m\},
\qquad m\ge c_wD,
\]

with

\[
1<|x_i-y_i|\le1+{K_w\over D}
\]

and both endpoints at depth at least `w` in one macroscopic inactive chamber. Moving
one chosen endpoint `x_i` straight toward its mate `y_i` is protected from the positive
core, the exterior source, and the diameter constraint until either the mate reaches
unit distance or another chamber isolate reaches unit distance first.

This note proves that the latter blocker events cannot concentrate on a bounded set of
isolates.

---

## 1. One blocker serves at most six pair-closing motions

For each matching pair orient it by choosing one endpoint `x_i` to move toward the
fixed mate `y_i`. Run the canonical Section-87 pair-closing motion separately for each
`i`.

If an isolate `z_i` different from `x_i,y_i` is the first obstruction, write `x_i'`
for the blocked position. Then

\[
|x_i'-z_i|=1,
\qquad
|x_i-x_i'|<|x_i-y_i|-1\le {K_w\over D}.
\tag{BM.1}
\]

### Theorem BM.1 — blocker multiplicity at most six

For all sufficiently large `D`, one fixed chamber isolate `z` can be the first blocker
for at most six of the selected pair-closing motions.

### Proof

Suppose `z` blocks the motions indexed by a set `I`. Every blocked position lies on the
unit circle about `z`:

\[
|x_i'-z|=1
\qquad(i\in I).
\tag{BM.2}
\]

The original moving endpoints `x_i` are distinct source points. Since all chamber
points in Section 87 are contact-isolated,

\[
|x_i-x_j|>1
\qquad(i\ne j).
\tag{BM.3}
\]

By (BM.1),

\[
|x_i'-x_j'|
\ge |x_i-x_j|-|x_i-x_i'|-|x_j-x_j'|
>1-{2K_w\over D}.
\tag{BM.4}
\]

Seven points on a unit circle always contain two whose smaller angular separation is at
most `2pi/7`. Their chord distance is therefore at most

\[
2\sin{\pi\over7}=0.867767\ldots<1.
\tag{BM.5}
\]

Choose `D` so large that

\[
1-{2K_w\over D}>2\sin{\pi\over7}.
\tag{BM.6}
\]

Then (BM.4) contradicts (BM.5) if `|I|>=7`. Hence `|I|<=6`. `square`

The constant six is deliberately conservative. Six nearly regular positions on the
unit circle are compatible with the estimate (BM.4), so no improvement to five is
claimed without additional geometry.

---

## 2. Blocked triples have a controlled local angle

Assume `z_i` blocks before the mate reaches unit distance. Put the blocked position at
the origin and orient the positive `x` axis toward the mate. Write

\[
y_i=r e,
\qquad 1<r\le1+{K_w\over D},
\]

and

\[
z_i=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)
\]

after reflecting across the pair line if necessary. Let

\[
t=|x_i-x_i'|,
\qquad 0<t<{K_w\over D},
\]

so the original moving point is `x_i=-te`.

Because both `z_i` and `y_i` are original contact isolates,

\[
|z_i-x_i|>1,
\qquad
|z_i-y_i|>1.
\]

Squaring gives

\[
1+t^2+2t\cos\theta>1,
\]

and

\[
1+r^2-2r\cos\theta>1.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
-{t\over2}<\cos\theta<{r\over2}.
}
\tag{BM.7}
\]

In particular, uniformly over all blocked gadgets,

\[
{\pi\over3}-O(D^{-1})
<\theta
<{\pi\over2}+O(D^{-1}).
\tag{BM.8}
\]

Thus every first-block event is a controlled near-triangular/right-angle wedge, not an
arbitrary near-contact.

---

## 3. Linear disjoint activation gadgets

Classify each matching pair as

- **direct** if the mate reaches unit distance before any other point blocks; or
- **blocked** otherwise.

The original matching pairs are vertex-disjoint.

### Corollary BM.2 — linear direct pairs or linear disjoint blocked triples

There is an absolute constant `c'_w>0`, depending only on the Section-87 constants,
such that every sufficiently large source in the canonical internal inactive-chamber
branch has one of the following:

1. at least `c'_wD` vertex-disjoint deep pairs whose protected straight motion reaches
   an exact mate contact without any intervening blocker; or
2. at least `c'_wD` pairwise vertex-disjoint triples
   \[
   \{x_i,y_i,z_i\}
   \]
   in which `z_i` is the first blocker of the protected `x_i -> y_i` motion and the
   blocked geometry obeys (BM.1), (BM.7), and (BM.8).

One may take, for example,

\[
c'_w={c_w\over42}.
\tag{BM.9}
\]

### Proof

If at least half of the `m>=c_wD` pairs are direct, the first alternative holds with
`c'_w=c_w/2`.

Otherwise at least `m/2` pairs are blocked. Form a 3-uniform hypergraph whose edge for
a blocked pair is

\[
E_i=\{x_i,y_i,z_i\}.
\]

A source point belongs to at most one matching pair, so it appears as a moving/mate
endpoint in at most one hyperedge. By Theorem BM.1 it appears as a blocker in at most
six hyperedges. Thus every vertex has hypergraph degree at most seven.

Greedily choose one hyperedge and discard all hyperedges meeting it. One selected edge
meets at most

\[
3\cdot7=21
\]

hyperedges, including itself. Hence the blocked hypergraph contains a matching of size
at least

\[
{m/2\over21}={m\over42}
\ge {c_w\over42}D.
\]

These are pairwise vertex-disjoint blocked triples. `square`

---

## 4. What this changes

Section 87 left a potentially severe local concentration regression: linearly many
pair-closing attempts might all have been stopped by the same few interior rattlers.
That is impossible. The deep inactive packing contains a **linear disjoint population
of local activation gadgets**.

This does not yet supply the finite `k -> k+1` packing exchange required by the
stress-transversal reinsertion theorem. In particular:

- creating linearly many new isolate-isolate contacts is not itself a contradiction;
- a zero-reserve near-triangular chamber can still realize many such gadgets;
- the theorem gives no lower bound on the sum of the original pair gaps; and
- the gadgets need not align in one common direction.

The next useful chamber theorem must exploit the disjoint gadget population
collectively: either construct an augmenting/repacking path that produces one protected
spare site, or show that a positive density of blocked wedges forces a contact pattern
that reaches the active chamber boundary.

---

## 5. Scope

- The result is only for the canonical internal inactive-chamber branch and uses the
  canonical Section-87 deep matching.
- All motions are the individually protected motions already certified by Section 87.
- No source point is deleted in the theorem.
- Contact isolates remain outside the positive-core affine-kernel theorem.
- The blocked positions `x_i'` are auxiliary geometric positions, not asserted source
  points of the original minimizer.
- No inference from contact creation to KKT force transfer is made.

**Disposition:** proof-only structural theorem. C-II remains open.